# curl's own ignore conventions, and what this package preserves of them.
#
# The first fact, because it decides the shape of everything below: there is no
# upstream ignore file here to preserve or to remove. Listing the pristine
# extracted `curl-8.22.0` tree in the driver's import scratch -- 4406 files,
# searched at every depth -- finds no `.gitignore`, no `.gitattributes` and no
# `.ignore`, and the same search over the vendored subset finds none either.
# curl's *repository* does carry an ignore file; a repository file is not part
# of a release tarball, so it was not read here and nothing in it is reproduced
# or dropped. Every entry below is this fork's own.
#
# What upstream's conventions *do* survive into this tree is its build-system
# file names, and those are preserved without exception. curl ships three build
# systems and this fork executes none of them, but all of their files are
# vendored, unmodified, with digests in UPSTREAM.sha256:
#
#   lib/Makefile.am        lib/Makefile.in         lib/Makefile.inc
#   lib/Makefile.soname    include/Makefile.am     include/Makefile.in
#   include/curl/Makefile.am
#   lib/CMakeLists.txt     lib/curl_config.h.in    lib/curl_config-cmake.h.in
#   lib/libcurl.vers.in    lib/libcurl.def         lib/libcurl.rc
#   lib/optiontable.pl
#
# That list is also the reason for the patterns this file does *not* contain.
# Each of these is a common enough entry in a C project's ignore file, and each
# would silently swallow vendored upstream material that this package ships:
#
#   `*.in`        lib/Makefile.in, include/Makefile.in, include/curl/Makefile.in,
#                 lib/curl_config.h.in, lib/curl_config-cmake.h.in,
#                 lib/libcurl.vers.in
#   `*.def`       lib/libcurl.def, the Windows export list
#   `*.rc`        lib/libcurl.rc, the Windows version resource
#   `*.pl`        lib/optiontable.pl, the generator for the option tables
#   `Makefile*`   all five upstream Makefiles above, and lib/CMakeLists.txt is
#                 not a Makefile but is close enough that a careless pattern
#                 would take it too
#   `config.h`    nothing vendored is named that -- but see below, where the one
#                 genuinely generated header gets its own entry
#
# None of them is here. Nothing below re-includes a vendored file either,
# because a `!` line for a file no pattern excludes is a no-op that reads like
# a safeguard and is not one; the safeguard here is the absence of the broad
# patterns, and the audit surface that would catch a mistake is
# `sha256sum -c UPSTREAM.sha256` over 418 records.
#
# A `.gitignore` keeps untracked noise out of a commit. It is not what keeps
# anything out of a *package*: that is the `.paths` allow list in
# `build.zig.zon`, which is why nothing generated, fetched or tool-managed is
# named there either. `.github/workflows/verify.yml` is deliberately outside
# `.paths`, matching the sibling SDKs in this workspace -- a verification
# workflow is not a dependency a dependent receives, and it is not an artefact
# of the library.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: curl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- upstream
#
# The output names curl's own autotools and libtool build would create in this
# tree. This package never runs `configure` -- the configure-generated input is
# produced instead by a host-target C generator from the target's facts -- so
# these entries are defensive: their purpose is that a stray in-tree autotools
# run cannot litter a tree that is built with Zig, and cannot commit a
# `Makefile` that shadows the real build graph.
Makefile
Makefile.in.in
aclocal.m4
autom4te.cache/
config.cache
config.guess
config.h
config.log
config.status
config.sub
libtool
stamp-h1
.deps/
.libs/
*.la
*.lo

# `lib/curl_config.h` is generated twice upstream -- by `configure` from
# `lib/curl_config.h.in` and by CMake from `lib/curl_config-cmake.h.in` -- and
# named with its own entry because the pattern `config.h` above does not match
# it. In this package it is generated for the *target being built* into the Zig
# cache, never into the source tree, so this entry is defensive in the same way.
lib/curl_config.h

# The output names curl's CMake build would create. `lib/CMakeLists.txt` is
# vendored upstream material and none of these patterns matches it.
CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
CMakeUserPresets.json
cmake_install.cmake
Makefile.cmake

# Compiler and archiver output. curl's own Windows build also produces import
# libraries and debug symbols; `*.lib`, `*.exp`, `*.pdb` and `*.dll` cover
# those names. None of them matches a vendored file: the closest is
# `lib/libcurl.rc`, which is upstream's version resource and is not listed.
*.o
*.obj
*.a
*.so
*.so.*
*.dll
*.lib
*.exp
*.pdb
*.exe
*.d
*.gcno
*.gcda
*.swp

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- zig
#
# `zig build` artefacts (fork addition). These are directories rather than
# single files because a normal `zig build` creates them, and a normal
# `zig build` must not dirty the tree or leak into the package published from
# it. None of them is named in build.zig.zon `.paths`, so an ignored directory
# can never become part of the package.
.zig-cache/
zig-out/

# Zig 0.16 fetches dependencies into a project-local directory (observed with
# Zig 0.16; not rechecked on 0.17). This package declares exactly one
# dependency and it is a *relative path* to the sibling mbed-TLS package, so
# nothing is fetched for it -- but a project-local fetch directory must not be
# committable either, in case that ever changes.
zig-pkg/

# The consumer package builds inside this repository and has its own cache and
# output tree. It is ignored here as well as in consumer/.gitignore so a build
# run from the repository root cannot commit it by accident.
consumer/.zig-cache/
consumer/zig-out/
consumer/zig-pkg/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- nix
#
# `nix build` writes `result` (and `result-1`, `result-2`, ... on a rebuild)
# into the directory that asked for it, and `devenv` writes its resolved
# inputs and its state beside the project. None of them is named in
# build.zig.zon `.paths`, so an ignored path can never become part of the
# package either. `devenv.lock` and `flake.lock` are deliberately *not* here:
# they are real inputs, they belong to the driver, and ignoring them would be
# the wrong way to keep a build out of a commit.
result
result-*
.devenv/
.devenv.flake.nix
.devenv.flakes/
devenv.local.nix
.direnv/
.envrc.local
# ------------------------------------------------------------------- qemu
#
# A cross-compiled test binary run under `qemu-<arch>` writes its core dump
# into the directory the build was launched from, which is the package root,
# under the name `qemu_<test-binary>_<timestamp>_<pid>.core`. A target that
# faults under emulation therefore leaves one 83 MiB file per crashed test in
# the source tree - 52 of them, 4.2 GiB, from one 32-bit big-endian MIPS run.
# They are build output, not source, and nothing names them in
# `build.zig.zon` `.paths`.
*.core
qemu_*.core
